Janis Papanagnou wrote:
> archmage84 wrote:
>
>> And have you _seen_ Jumping at expert? It's four squares in one turn.
>
> The point is; have you often perceived that 4 vs. 2 squares is a *huge*
> advantage in practice that it's worth to spend (or rather waste) skill
> slots on that? Really?

If I'm in such a crowd that jumping 4 will get me out of trouble, and
I'm in a lit area where I can see that far, I had such agressive playing
style to get into that trouble that one change like jumping will not
help me survive that game.

Where jumping range matters the most would be the sanctum and the astral
plane.  I am not there much of the time. When I'm there I'm in a very
big hurry to get past the crowd and extra range would be worth the
extra skill slots in preparation.

Checking the table only wizards can get to expert in the escape school. 
When I tabulated what schools I'd want to advance with a wizard I
figured I'd end up pushing every spell school to max.  Wizards want to
get to XL=30 because of the increased damage from magic missiles
(arguable point I understand) so they have all 29 to work with.  After I
subtract the 6 for dagger and a couple for good artifact backup
secondary wields, I was still left with enough slots to push all or
almost all of the spell schools to maximum.

Some spell schools aren't worth enhancing after you're at 0% failure
rate.  Some spell schools are worth enhancing for the change in
behavior.  With wizards it can boil down to it being an issue that the
skill slots are available for the use with no plans on every using the
slots on anything else.

Here's what I ended up concluding about wizard skill slot strategy:

A wizard character at XP 30 should be able to #enhance all spell
schools to maximum, dagger to Expert, chaotic-broadsword or
neutral-long-sword to Basic, and still have room to #enhance 3 slots
worth of other items.

Not all wizards put in the effort to get the weapon at crowning or get
crowned at all.  Some wizards lose interest in altar camping as soon as
they have Magicbane, so even the slot for broadsword or long sword
might not be used. A few wizards need to enhance quarterstaff in the
early game for survival.

The less wizards are interested in variety weapons the more they have
available to max out every spell school.  For the fun of demonstrating
that to myself I've played a wizard who maxed out dagger for Magicbane
and the quiver, broadsword for Stormy as a backup, pole arms to have fun
using a spetum at both castles, all spell schools.  A bit of care was
needed in the early game to not enhance the quarterstaff and as soon as
I got a non-stacking elven dagger to name I ignored the rest of the
weapon schools.


